VVEERRSSIIOONNSS
       On some architectures —for example, x86‐64 and ARM— there is no ssoocckkeett‐‐
       ccaallll()  system  call;  instead ssoocckkeett(2), aacccceepptt(2), bbiinndd(2), and so on
       really are implemented as separate system calls.

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
       Linux.

       On x86‐32, ssoocckkeettccaallll() was historically the only entry point  for  the
       sockets  API.   However, starting in Linux 4.3, direct system calls are
       provided on x86‐32 for the sockets API.  This facilitates the  creation
       of  sseeccccoommpp(2)  filters that filter sockets system calls (for new user‐
       space binaries that are compiled to use the new entry points) and  also
       provides a (very) small performance improvement.

